\section{Results}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/attenuation.pdf}
\caption{Added value of patient metadata $\hat\Delta$ (out-of-fold log-loss reduction, $10^{-3}$ nats) along the ladder for all eight image sets, with 95\% grouped-bootstrap CIs (fold assignment 0, all images). Panels have different y-axis scales: PAD-UFES-20's values are about two orders of magnitude larger. HSV: colour histogram; R18/R50: ResNet-18/50; CNX-T: ConvNeXt-T; ViT-B: ViT-B/16.}\label{fig:atten}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Pre-registered analysis on HAM10000}\label{sec:ham}
\input{tables/ham_ladder}
Fig.~\ref{fig:atten} and Table~\ref{tab:ham} show that metadata reduce out-of-fold log-loss by $\HAMavHSV\times10^{-3}$ nats for colour histograms, with a CI excluding zero in all three fold assignments. For the deep representations the reduction is only $\HAMavResEighteen\times10^{-3}$ to $\HAMavConvNeXt\times10^{-3}$ nats, and the log-loss CIs include zero. Table~\ref{tab:hamrobust} expresses the same effect in familiar units: the AUROC gain falls from \HAMdaucHSV{} \HAMdaucHSVCI{} for colour histograms to \HAMdaucResEighteen{} \HAMdaucResEighteenCI{} for ResNet-18, which remains detectable, and to \HAMdaucConvNeXt{} \HAMdaucConvNeXtCI{} for ConvNeXt-T, which does not. Fitting all frozen features and metadata in one model gives nearly the same log-loss as the two-stage design (Table~\ref{tab:hamrobust}), so compressing the image to a one-dimensional score does not hide an interaction.

Recoverability of age from the image rises along the ladder ($R^2$ from \HAMageRecovHSV{} to \HAMageRecovConvNeXt{}), consistent with Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} for metadata whose information the image carries. A per-variable analysis did not sharpen this: age, sex, and site retained \HAMretainedAge{}, \HAMretainedSex{}, and \HAMretainedSite{} of their colour-histogram added value at the strongest rungs, and even sex proved recoverable from dermoscopy (AUROC \HAMsexRecovConvNeXt{}). Because all three variables became recoverable together, HAM10000 alone cannot isolate the mechanism, which motivates the symptom analysis on PAD-UFES-20 below. Three of four pre-registered predictions held; the predicted non-zero floor was not observed.

\input{tables/ham_robust}

\subsection{Shortcut control and paired tests}\label{sec:shortcut}
\input{tables/ham_paired}
With image source included in both models, added value still falls (from $\HAMadjHSV\times10^{-3}$ to $\HAMadjConvNeXt\times10^{-3}$ nats). Within single sources, melanoma AUROC rises with capacity, so the deep representations do read lesions and do not merely detect the source. The paired attenuation from ResNet-18 to ConvNeXt-T is $\HAMpairedRConv\times10^{-3}$ nats (95\% CI \HAMpairedRConvCI{}$\times10^{-3}$). Across all \PERMn{} image sets, the real metadata outperformed every one of \PERMR{} group-level permutations at ConvNeXt-T on complete cases (empirical $p=\PERMp$, the smallest attainable), so the residual added value is not an artefact of fitting extra parameters.

\subsection{Pre-registered replication}\label{sec:rep}
\input{tables/replication}
Table~\ref{tab:rep} and Fig.~\ref{fig:atten} report both waves. After Holm correction within each registered family, H1 (colour histogram vs.\ ConvNeXt-T) held in \REPwaveOneHone{} of 3 datasets in wave~1 and \REPwaveTwoHone{} of 4 image sets in wave~2, that is, in all \REPindepHone{} independent datasets (MILK10k counted once, and as holding only if both of its image sets held). H2 (ResNet-18 vs.\ ConvNeXt-T) held in \REPwaveOneHtwo{} of 3 and \REPwaveTwoHtwo{} of 4 (MSK 2020: $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=\REPmskHtwoP$), i.e.\ in \REPindepHtwo{} of \REPindepN{} independent datasets. With the strictly cross-fitted estimator, H1 held in \ESTindepHone{} and H2 in \ESTindepHtwo{} of \REPindepN{}: ISIC 2020's H2 moved from $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=\ESTisicHtwoOrig$ to \ESTisicHtwoCorr{} on a difference of $\ESTisicHtwoDiff\times10^{-3}$ nats. Across fold assignments, patient weighting, alternative label definitions, complete cases and missing-data encodings, H1 failed only where the metadata carried almost no information (MILK10k, melanoma vs.\ rest: $\LABmilkIA\times10^{-3}$ nats), whereas H2 depended on the estimator, the label, the fold assignment and the weighting (Appendix~\ref{app:robust}). At ConvNeXt-T the remaining added value ($10^{-3}$ nats, 95\% CI) is: \REPfloors{}.

\textbf{Mechanism test 1 (PAD-UFES-20, wave 1).} We predicted that symptoms, being invisible in photographs, would keep their value while demographics attenuated. From HSV to ConvNeXt-T, the demographic block retained \PADmechD{} of its added value and the symptom block \PADmechS{}, so neither M1 nor M2 held. The premise was false: even \emph{within} a diagnostic class, ConvNeXt-T features predict the symptoms with AUROC \PADsymWithinMin{} to \PADsymWithinMax{} (bleeding and elevation are visible).

\subsection{Pre-registered modality and lesion-size tests}\label{sec:p1}
\textbf{Mechanism test 2 (P1, MILK10k).} If metadata substitute for image detail, clinical close-ups, which show less detail than dermoscopy, should leave more of the metadata's information non-redundant. On the same \PoneN{} lesions the opposite held: $\rho=\PoneRhoClin$ for clinical vs.\ \PoneRhoDerm{} for dermoscopic images at ConvNeXt-T, a paired difference of \PoneDiff{} \PoneCI{}. P1 is therefore rejected. A plausible reason is that its premise again failed in our setting: the two image types gave almost identical image-only AUROC (\MILKaucClin{} vs.\ \MILKaucDerm{}), so the clinical representation was not weaker, consistent with a reader study that found the two modalities complementary rather than ranked~\cite{muller2026milk}.

\textbf{P2 (MSK 2020).} Measured lesion diameter added $\PtwoHSV\times10^{-3}$ nats \PtwoHSVCI{} to the colour-histogram model but only $\PtwoCNX\times10^{-3}$ \PtwoCNXCI{} to ConvNeXt-T, a retained share of \PtwoRetained{} (registered threshold $<0.5$). P2 was registered as exploratory because MSK 2020 is small; the result is consistent with the finding below that deep image scores track lesion size.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/retained.pdf}
\caption{Retained fraction $\rho$ of the metadata's standalone information left non-redundant by ConvNeXt-T, per image set. Circles: all images with 95\% grouped-bootstrap CI (Table~\ref{tab:retained}); diamonds: complete cases (Table~\ref{tab:cc}).}\label{fig:retained}
\end{figure}
\input{tables/retained}

\subsection{How much information remains?}
Normalising $\hat\Delta$ by the metadata's standalone information $\hat I_A$ gives the retained fraction $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:retained}, Fig.~\ref{fig:retained}). It falls steeply from colour histograms to the deep representations everywhere, but its level at ConvNeXt-T varies from \RHOmin{} (\RHOminDS{}) to \RHOmax{} (\RHOmaxDS{}). Dermoscopic datasets lie at both ends of this range, so the floor is a property of the dataset, its label definition and its metadata, not of the imaging modality. The label matters directly: on ISIC 2019, $\rho$ is \LABisicMelRho{} for melanoma but \LABisicMalRho{} \LABisicMalRhoCI{} for malignant vs.\ benign (corrected estimator). Where added value is small, weighting and encoding matter too: HIBA's value at ConvNeXt-T is $\WGThibaImg$ per image but $\WGThibaGrp$ per patient, and $\ENCHibaNoInd$ without missing-value indicators ($10^{-3}$ nats).

\subsection{Complete-case sensitivity analysis}\label{sec:cc}
\input{tables/complete_case}
Restricting every dataset to images with all metadata recorded (Table~\ref{tab:cc}) removes label-informative missingness. H1 holds (uncorrected) in \CCholdHone{} of \CCn{} image sets and H2 in \CCholdHtwo{} (not in \CCfailHtwo{}). On PAD-UFES-20, the standalone information in the metadata falls from $\PADIAleak\times10^{-3}$ to $\PADIAcc\times10^{-3}$ nats, so much of that dataset's apparent metadata signal reflects how missing values arise; its complete cases (\PADccKept{} images, mostly biopsied) are, however, a different population. Dropping the missing-value indicators instead, with median or multiple imputation fitted in the training fold, halved PAD-UFES-20's added value at ConvNeXt-T ($\ENCPadInd$ to $\ENCPadNoInd$ and $\ENCPadMI\times10^{-3}$ nats) but raised HIBA's ($\ENCHibaInd$ to $\ENCHibaNoInd$); H1's direction held under every encoding. Complete-case retained fractions at ConvNeXt-T range from \CCRHOmin{} to \CCRHOmax{}.

\subsection{What makes a representation ``strong''?}\label{sec:ft}
\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose.pdf}
\caption{Exploratory dose--response on HAM10000 (ResNet-18): added value of metadata vs.\ the share of training lesions available to the image model. All runs on complete cases. Blue: fine-tuned end to end; line through the mean of runs (two subsamples below 100\%), small dots and bars are individual runs with 95\% CIs, numbers are mean image AUROC. Orange: frozen ImageNet features with a retrained head (mean of two subsamples).}\label{fig:dose}
\end{figure}
\input{tables/finetune}
The ladder orders representations by image-only performance but changes architecture, size, and pretraining at once. Four exploratory manipulations separate these factors; their CIs are not corrected for multiplicity.

\textbf{Fine-tuning.} ResNet-18 and ConvNeXt-T were fine-tuned end to end with the same grouped folds and a fixed schedule (AdamW, learning rate $10^{-4}$, six epochs, no early stopping on test folds). On HAM10000, fine-tuning ResNet-18 raised image AUROC to \FThamAUC{} and lowered added value from $\FThamFrozen$ to $\FTham\times10^{-3}$ nats \FThamCI{}, a paired drop of $\FThamDiff\times10^{-3}$ \FThamDiffCI{} (Table~\ref{tab:ft}). For ConvNeXt-T, whose frozen added value was already small, the drop was not detectable \FTcnxDiffCI{}. On HIBA and PAD-UFES-20 the drop was significant for both backbones, although on PAD-UFES-20 most of the value remained (ConvNeXt-T: $\FTpadCNXfrozen$ to $\FTpadCNXft\times10^{-3}$ nats). On ISIC 2019 and MILK10k (dermoscopic; ResNet-18 only), fine-tuning raised image AUROC (\FTIsicAUCfrz{} to \FTIsicAUCft{}; \FTMilkAUCfrz{} to \FTMilkAUCft{}) but added value fell only slightly ($\FTIsicFrozen$ to $\FTIsicFt$; $\FTMilkFrozen$ to $\FTMilkFt\times10^{-3}$ nats), and neither paired difference excluded zero (\FTIsicDiffCI{}; \FTMilkDiffCI{}).

\textbf{Training-set size.} On HAM10000's complete cases, ResNet-18 was fine-tuned on 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, and 100\% of each training fold's lesions, with two independent subsamples below 100\% (\FTfracNruns{} runs). Mean added value fell from $\FTfracTen$ to $\FTfracTwentyFive$, $\FTfracFifty$, and $\FTfracFull\times10^{-3}$ nats (100\%: 95\% CI \FTfracFullCI{}) as image AUROC rose from \FTfracTenAUC{} to \FTfracFullAUC{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}); across runs, the Spearman correlation between training fraction and added value was \FTfracSpearman{}. Between 50\% and 100\%, image AUROC changed little (\FTfracFiftyAUC{} vs.\ \FTfracFullAUC{}) while added value kept falling, although the intervals of adjacent fractions overlap; image AUROC is thus an incomplete summary of representation strength. Retraining only the head of frozen features on the same fractions moved added value in the same direction but less ($\FZhamTen$ to $\FZhamFull$ on HAM10000; $\FZisicTen$ to $\FZisicFull$ on ISIC 2019).

\textbf{Backbone size.} Within families (ResNet-18/34/50/101; ConvNeXt-T/S/B), frozen and on complete cases of \SIZEdatasets{} datasets, none of \SIZEn{} smallest-vs-largest comparisons of added value was significant, and image AUROC changed by only \SIZEaucChangeMin{} to \SIZEaucChangeMax{}.

\textbf{A larger pretrained model.} A frozen ViT-L/16 (304M parameters, weakly supervised SWAG pretraining~\cite{singh2022swag}) changed image AUROC relative to ConvNeXt-T by \VITLaucMin{} to \VITLaucMax{} across \VITLn{} image sets; added value was slightly lower on \VITLlower{} and not higher anywhere.

Together, these results tie attenuation to how well the image model solves the task, not to the number of parameters applied to frozen features.

\subsection{Erasing what the image encodes about age}\label{sec:erase}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/erasure.pdf}
\caption{Exploratory concept erasure (ConvNeXt-T): added value of age as the linear trace of age is removed from the image representation (dose $t$; $t=0$: unchanged features). Diamonds and squares at $t=1$: random subspaces whose removal costs as much image performance (matched) or has the same rank (same-rank); means over five draws. MILK10k is on its own vertical scale; the other panels share one. Strictly cross-fitted estimator, fold assignment 0.}\label{fig:erasure}
\end{figure*}
\input{tables/erasure}
Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} predicts that if a variable adds little because the representation already encodes it, removing that encoding should restore its value. We tested this with linear concept erasure~\cite{belrose2023leace}: in each training fold, the closed-form projection that makes age linearly undecodable was fitted to the image features and applied with dose $t$ (exploratory; registered before running). One direction sufficed to make age undecodable, and age's added value then rose monotonically with dose on the three datasets where age was decodable (HAM10000: $\ERASEhamT$ to $\ERASEhamOne$; MILK10k: $\ERASEmilkT$ to $\ERASEmilkOne\times10^{-3}$ nats; Fig.~\ref{fig:erasure}, Table~\ref{tab:erasure}). The rise is not a generic effect of a weaker image model: removing random subspaces of the same rank changed nothing, and removing random subspaces large enough to cost as much image performance raised age's value far less; the paired difference from that matched control excluded zero in \ERASEheld{} of \ERASEn{} dataset--backbone pairs, namely every pair on the three datasets where age was decodable (on MILK10k the matched control cost somewhat less image performance than erasure: log-loss \ERASEmilkLLmatched{} vs.\ \ERASEmilkLLconcept{}, ConvNeXt-T). On PAD-UFES-20, where age was barely decodable ($R^2=\ERASEpadRsq$), erasure had no effect. The effect was specific to the erased variable (HAM10000: age $+\ERASEhamDelta$, site $+\ERASEhamSite$, sex $+\ERASEhamSex$), and erasing age cost image performance (log-loss \ERASEhamLLzero{} to \ERASEhamLLone{}), so the image models use age-related features for diagnosis. Sex was decodable but carried no added value, and erasing it restored none. These results show the substitution term of Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} at work for age; they concern linear information only and do not show that training learns age \emph{in order to} diagnose.

\subsection{Where do metadata help?}\label{sec:where}
Pre-registered before analysis, we built for each training fold a nearest-neighbour graph of the image representation and asked whether per-image benefit is larger where a lesion's neighbours have discordant training labels (H1), where the image model is uncertain (H2), and whether graph statistics improve held-out prediction of benefit beyond the fitted models (H4; Appendix~\ref{app:where}). At ConvNeXt-T, H2 held in \GRPhTwo{} of 7 dataset units, as the bound $u(s)\le H(Y\mid S=s)$ requires; H1 held in \GRPhOne{} (registered threshold: 5) and H4 in \GRPhFour{}. Post hoc, the fusion model's label-free expected gain $K$ ranked per-image benefit best in \GRPkBest{} of 8 image sets, and the quarter of images with the largest $K$ carried \GRPcapMin{}--\GRPcapMax{} of the total benefit (HIBA excluded: total near zero), although $K$ overstated the level of benefit, by up to \KoverDmax{}-fold. On ConvNeXt-T, benefit showed no local organisation on the graph beyond label and image uncertainty (normalised Dirichlet energy at most \SMOOTHcnxMax{} below a stratified permutation null, significant in \SMOOTHcnxSig{} of 8 sets; colour histograms: \SMOOTHhsvSig{}). In the decomposition $\Delta=H\eta$, falling image uncertainty accounted for only \DECshareMin{}--\DECshareMax{} of the log-attenuation from colour histograms to ConvNeXt-T on seven sets (bootstrap upper bounds $\le\DECshareUpper$), against \DECpad{} \DECpadCI{} on PAD-UFES-20 (exploratory): strong representations attenuate metadata mainly by leaving uncertainty that metadata no longer resolve, not by resolving more cases.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\textbf{What the results mean.} On every independent dataset, metadata add clearly more to a colour-histogram model than to frozen deep representations, and better task-specific image models (fine-tuned, or trained on more data) leave less for the metadata to add, although on ISIC 2019 and MILK10k the drop after fine-tuning was small and not significant. Attenuation \emph{between} frozen deep representations is smaller and inconsistent (H2: \REPindepHtwo{} of \REPindepN{}), as expected if those representations differ little in task performance. How much value remains at a strong representation depends on the dataset and its label definition, from little (HAM10000; HIBA, depending on weighting and missing-value encoding) to about half (PAD-UFES-20, MSK 2020, ISIC 2019 for melanoma), and not on imaging modality: the pre-registered prediction that clinical images leave more value than dermoscopy of the same lesions failed. Concept erasure shows the redundancy at work for age: the value age loses to strong representations returns when their age information is removed. The frequently reported benefit of metadata~\cite{hohn2021review,pacheco2020impact} is therefore partly a statement about weak image representations, and the size of the part that is not differs severalfold between datasets.

\textbf{Which variables keep their value?} In an exploratory analysis over \RECrows{} dataset--representation--variable combinations (of \RECrowsAll{}; the rest had no significant standalone value), variables that the image predicts well within a diagnostic class kept a smaller share of their value (Spearman $\RECrho$; partial correlation adjusting for standalone value and image AUROC $\RECpartial$, dataset-clustered 95\% CI \RECpartialCI{}; between \RECrungMin{} and \RECrungMax{} within each representation). This is the pattern Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} predicts, but it is correlational, and it holds only among variables with demonstrable standalone value: there it survives a mixed model with dataset effects (coefficient \RECfiltMixed{}), whereas over all \RECallRows{} combinations it weakens (partial \RECallPartial{}; mixed \RECallMixed{}). A transparent rule fitted to these quantities did not predict a variable's added value in held-out datasets better than predicting zero (mean absolute error \RULEridge{} vs.\ \RULEzero{} nats); the simple product of standalone value and a typical retained fraction did best (\RULEbase{}). As a yes/no decision (collect if the predicted value is at least $10^{-3}$ nats), that simple product was right in \RULEbaseAcc{} of cases (precision \RULEbasePrec{}, recall \RULEbaseRec{}; Appendix~\ref{app:explor}). With six independent datasets, a reliable quantitative rule is beyond reach.

\textbf{Under dataset shift.} When the image and fusion models were fitted on one dataset and applied to another (harmonised age, sex, and site; \SHIFTn{} directed pairs), metadata improved log-loss significantly in \SHIFThelp{} pairs and worsened it in \SHIFThurt{}, from $\SHIFTmin$ to $\SHIFTmax$ nats. The largest harms occurred between datasets with very different prevalences and label definitions, so these results describe the joint shift rather than a property of metadata. Even within one dataset, added value learned on some sources did not transfer: holding out one HAM10000 source at a time (all images, strictly cross-fitted, ConvNeXt-T, $10^{-3}$ nats): \SRChamText{}; ISIC 2019, BCN to MSK $\SRCbcnMsk$.

\textbf{Practical implication.} Metadata fusion should be evaluated against the strongest available image model, ideally one fine-tuned on the target data, and the retained fraction $\rho$ reported alongside the gain, so that a benefit that merely compensates for a weak backbone is not mistaken for complementary information. Because benefit is concentrated, the fusion model's expected gain can rank the lesions whose metadata matter most, although it overstates the size of the gain.

\textbf{Limitations.} (i) Fine-tuning was examined on five datasets (two with ResNet-18 only) with one training run per fold and a fixed schedule; the drop in added value was significant on three, and fine-tuned scores were not re-estimated with the strictly cross-fitted second stage. (ii) $\hat\Delta$ estimates the reduction achievable by a logistic second stage on a one-dimensional score, not $I(Y;A\mid S)$ itself. (iii) The frozen ladder is ordered by image-only performance and is not a controlled intervention; Section~\ref{sec:ft} addresses this only in part. (iv) Both pre-registered mechanism tests failed because their premises (invisible symptoms; a weaker clinical-image representation) did not hold; the mechanism is shown by intervention only for age and only for linearly decodable information. (v) Images come from public archives and mirrors; official metadata were used and counts were checked against the published descriptions. MSK 2020 has no lesion identifier and is grouped per image (an embedding check found no near-duplicate images within it); ISIC 2019 images without one are grouped individually. (vi) Missingness is label-informative in every dataset, severely so in PAD-UFES-20; complete-case analysis removes this leak but analyses a selected subsample, and some exploratory PAD-UFES-20 results (Table~\ref{tab:ft}) use all images. (vii) All analyses are retrospective; nothing here is a clinical claim, and no causal effect of metadata is estimated. (viii) Bootstrap CIs hold the fitted models fixed and omit training variability~\cite{bates2024cv}: across fold assignments, added value at ConvNeXt-T varied by $\SPREADpad\times10^{-3}$ nats on PAD-UFES-20 (CI half-width \SPREADpadHW{}) and HIBA's took the values \SPREADhiba{}. (ix) Saliency analyses (Appendix~\ref{app:interp}) are evidence of spatial correspondence, not of causal reliance~\cite{adebayo2018sanity}.

\section{Conclusion}
The value of patient metadata to a skin lesion classifier is not fixed: it depends on what the image model already extracts. Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} makes this precise. Pre-registered experiments on seven datasets show that metadata add far less to deep representations than to colour histograms, a result that survives a strictly cross-fitted estimator, alternative labels, complete cases and missing-data encoding. Interventions show what drives it: fine-tuning and more training data tend to shrink the gain, more parameters on frozen features do not, and erasing what a representation encodes about age restores age's value. Attenuation among frozen deep representations did not replicate consistently, the share of metadata information left non-redundant ranged severalfold across datasets and with the label definition, imaging modality did not explain that range, and the remaining benefit concentrated in lesions the image model found uncertain. Claims that metadata improve a classifier should therefore be made against strong, task-adapted image baselines and accompanied by the retained fraction.

