\textbf{Recoverability} (Fig.~\ref{fig:recov}). The negative association reported in Section~VI does not depend on the colour histogram: restricted to the deep representations, the within-dataset Spearman correlation is negative in \RECdeepNeg{} of \RECdeepN{} image sets (from \RECdeepMin{} to \RECdeepMax{}; as few as \RECdeepMinRows{} points in some sets). The rule excluding rows with non-significant standalone value was fixed in the analysis code before the run but was not stated in the dated registry.

\textbf{Permutation control} (Table~\ref{tab:perm}). Permuting whole metadata records between grouping units destroys their link to the image and label but keeps their joint distribution; permuted metadata never matched the real metadata's added value and always had negative added value on average, as expected from fitting extra parameters to noise.

\textbf{Collection rule} (Table~\ref{tab:rule}). A ridge model on six cheap indicators was worse than predicting zero; the product of standalone value and the median retained share of the training datasets was the best predictor and made a useful collect/skip decision.

\textbf{Dataset shift} (Table~\ref{tab:shift}). Under shift, metadata fitted on the source frequently harmed the target, most strongly between datasets with very different prevalences. Within HAM10000, holding out one image source at a time ($10^{-3}$ nats, 95\% CI): \LOSOtext{}.

