\section{Where metadata help: details}\label{app:where}
\input{tables/app_graph}
For each outer training fold, image features were standardised with training-fold statistics and $L_2$-normalised; each training image was linked to its 20 nearest training images from other grouping units (cosine distance, Gaussian weights with bandwidth equal to the median 20th-neighbour distance, union-symmetrised), and each test image to its 20 nearest training images, so no test label entered any statistic. Local label entropy uses the neighbours' training labels; graph-local benefit averages the neighbours' inner out-of-fold benefit computed within the training fold. Results were the same with 10 or 50 neighbours and with binary weights. H4 compared ridge regressions with and without graph statistics, both including $K$, image entropy, margin, feature norm and local density; both had low held-out $R^2$, because their training targets came from models fitted on less data, so the post hoc rank correlations in Table~\ref{tab:graph} are the more informative comparison. Per-sample prediction of a feature's incremental value is established in dynamic feature selection~\cite{gadgil2024cmi}; these analyses ask only where the benefit lies.

\section{Interpretability}\label{app:interp}
\textbf{Interpretability test.} For a linear head $w$ on global-average-pooled features $f=\frac1{|\Omega|}\sum_{u\in\Omega}F(u)$, the class activation map $c(u)=\sum_j w_j F_j(u)$~\cite{zhou2016cam} decomposes the logit exactly. For a non-negative evidence map $e$ and a binary mask $m$ on a periodic grid $\Omega$, the mass inside the mask after a shift by $\tau$ is
\begin{equation}
M(\tau)=\frac{\sum_{u}e(u)\,m(u-\tau)}{\sum_u e(u)}=\frac{(e\star m)(\tau)}{\|e\|_1},
\end{equation}
a circular cross-correlation. Hence $\mathcal{F}^{-1}[\mathcal{F}(e)\overline{\mathcal{F}(m)}]$ yields $M(\tau)$ for all $|\Omega|$ shifts in $O(|\Omega|\log|\Omega|)$, and $|\{\tau: M(\tau)\ge M(0)\}|/|\Omega|$ is an exact p-value under the hypothesis that the mask's position is uniform over circular translations. Unlike pixel permutation, the shift null preserves the spatial autocorrelation of $e$.

\input{tables/app_swap}
\input{tables/interp}
\input{tables/interp_backbones}
For each backbone we use the exact class activation map of the out-of-fold head. For ConvNeXt-T, whose pooled features pass through a LayerNorm before the head, the map is exact up to a positive per-image factor: with per-image LayerNorm scale $\sigma$ and affine weights $\gamma$, the logit equals a constant plus $\sigma^{-1}|\Omega|^{-1}\sum_u\sum_j \tilde w_j\big(F_j(u)-\bar F(u)\big)$ with $\tilde w_j=w_j\gamma_j/s_j$ and $\bar F(u)$ the channel mean. The overlap statistics are invariant to that factor, and the reconstructed logits matched the head's logits with correlation $1.000$ for every backbone.

Mapping the \emph{predicted} class's evidence produced an apparent link between errors and off-lesion attention ($p<10^{-49}$; Table~\ref{tab:interp}). This is an artefact: for ``not melanoma'' predictions, the evidence for that class lies in the surrounding skin. With the melanoma-evidence map used for every image (Table~\ref{tab:interpbb}), melanoma evidence is concentrated on the lesion in \INTbbEnrichRange{} of images across the three backbones, a criterion that a centred map would also meet. Missed melanomas show no less on-lesion evidence; instead they are consistently smaller lesions ($p\le\INTbbAreaPmax$ for every backbone). Pixel permutation declares \INTbbPermRange{} of maps significant, against \INTbbShiftRange{} under the circular-shift null, consistent with the inflation predicted by its disregard of spatial autocorrelation~\cite{alexanderbloch2018,markello2022neuromaps}.

\textbf{The shift null is itself invalid here.} It is exact only if, given the map, the mask's position is uniform over circular translations. Dermoscopic lesions are centred, so any centred map passes it. A fixed centred Gaussian, which carries no image information, was significant for \GAUSSshift{} of images under the shift null and for \GAUSSswap{} under a mask-swap null that compares each image's own mask with the masks of 199 images of similar lesion area (E48; Table~\ref{tab:swap}). Under the swap null, \SWAPrange{} of real maps were significant (shift null on the same images: \SWAPshiftRange{}): some lesion-specific evidence exists, but most of the shift-null significance reflects centring. Preserving spatial autocorrelation is necessary for a valid spatial null but not sufficient; the transformation must also leave the joint distribution of map and mask unchanged under the null.

